If you are looking to improve math rendering in `opencode web` and support standard escaped LaTeX delimiters like `\(` and `\[` without leaking into inline code blocks, you can consider using browser-native MathML Core.

Since all major modern browser engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko) natively support MathML, compiling LaTeX to standard `<math>` tags delegates all layout work to the browser's native C++ engine, completely eliminating the need for client-side JS layout calculations and heavy CSS/font assets.

I wrote `@webc.site/math` (GitHub: https://github.com/webc-site/math, Live Demo: https://math.webc.site) — the world's smallest and fastest TeX-to-MathML compiler:
- Size: Only 3.58 KB Gzipped (zero dependencies).
- Speed: ~329,000 ops/s.

You can also use its plugin `@webc.site/math-marked` (or configure a custom marked extension) to parse TeX. To support `\(...\)` and `\[...\]` delimiters safely before parsing (while skipping inline/fenced code blocks), you can implement a custom tokenizer/lexer or pre-normalize them.

A simple pre-normalization function that skips code blocks (using regex to split code blocks from plain text) might look like:
```js
const normalizeDelimiters = (markdown) => {
  // Regex to split by fenced code blocks (```)
  const parts = markdown.split(/(```[\\s\\S]*?```)/g);
  return parts.map((part, index) => {
    // If it's a code block, leave it untouched
    if (index % 2 === 1) return part;
    // Otherwise, normalize \( ... \) and \[ ... \] to $ ... $ and $$ ... $$
    return part
      .replace(/\\\[([\\s\\S]*?)\\\]/g, '$$$$$1$$$$')
      .replace(/\\\(([\\s\\S]*?)\\\)/g, '$$$1$$');
  }).join('');
};
```
Applying this normalization before passing the markdown text to `marked` will cleanly resolve the delimiter issue without mutating any actual code samples.

